```

The problem emerges when `modelOverride` is empty or undefined and neither the Gmail hook nor model override is applied. In that case, the code falls back to `sessionEntry.modelOverride`, which should have been cleared for isolated cron sessions but apparently wasn't. This is where the unwanted model persistence happens.

When `sessionEntry.modelOverride` is set (likely carried over from a previous session), the code resolves it and overwrites the provider and model with the session's stored override instead of using the fresh model that was intended for this cron job.

The issue stems from how `resolveCronSession` handles new sessions with `forceNew: true` — it spreads the old session entry which includes the model override, then creates a fresh session with that stale state. I should check the heartbeat runner to see if it has the same problem.

